Incident/Problem/Change
2.3 Database Design Principles
Base Tables:
* All major objects inherit from base "Task" table
* Common fields: number, short_description, state, priority, assigned_to, created, updated
* Incidents, Requests, Problems, Changes all extend Task
CMDB Structure:
* CI base class with specialized subclasses
* Relationships table for CI connections
* Dependency mapping for impact analysis
Audit and History:
* Track all field changes
* Who changed what, when
* Full audit trail for compliance

3. Incident Management
3.1 Incident Lifecycle
Incident States:
1. New - Just submitted, not yet assigned
2. Assigned - Assigned to support group
3. In Progress - Agent actively working on it
4. On Hold - Waiting for information or external party
5. Resolved - Solution provided, awaiting user confirmation
6. Closed - User confirmed resolution or auto-closed after X days
3.2 Incident Fields (Standard Implementation)
Identifying Information:
* Number (auto-generated: INC0001234)
* Short Description (required, 160 chars)
* Description (detailed information, rich text)
* Caller (User who reported, required)
* Contact Type (Phone, Email, Walk-in, Self-Service, Monitoring)
* Opened At (timestamp)
* Opened By (user who created record)
Classification:
* Category (Hardware, Software, Network, Database, Security, etc.)
* Subcategory (Desktop, Laptop, Printer, etc.)
* Configuration Item (affected device/application)
* Service (affected IT service)
Prioritization:
* Impact (High, Medium, Low - how many users affected)
* Urgency (High, Medium, Low - how quickly needs resolution)
* Priority (auto-calculated from Impact × Urgency matrix)
    * P1 (Critical): High Impact + High Urgency
    * P2 (High): High Impact + Medium Urgency OR Medium Impact + High Urgency
    * P3 (Medium): Medium Impact + Medium Urgency
    * P4 (Low): Low Impact OR Low Urgency
Assignment:
* Assignment Group (support team, required when assigned)
* Assigned To (individual agent)
* Escalation Level (0, 1, 2, 3 for management escalation)
Resolution:
* State (New, Assigned, In Progress, On Hold, Resolved, Closed)
* Resolution Code (Solved Remotely, Solved at Desk, Hardware Repair, User Error, etc.)
* Resolution Notes (how it was fixed)
* Resolved At (timestamp)
* Resolved By (user)
* Closed At (timestamp)
* Closed By (user)
SLA Fields:
* SLA Breach (boolean - has SLA been violated)
* Time to Respond (deadline for first response)
* Time to Resolve (deadline for resolution)
* Business Elapsed Time (time spent in active states)
Additional Fields:
* Location (user's physical location)
* Company (for multi-tenant systems)
* Upon Approval (for incidents requiring approval)
* Upon Reject (action if approval denied)
* Parent Incident (for related/duplicate incidents)
* Problem (linked problem if root cause identified)
* Change Request (linked change if fix requires change)
3.3 Incident Routing and Assignment
Automatic Assignment Rules:
Rule 1: Category-Based Routing
IF Category = "Network" 
  THEN Assign to "Network Support Team"
  
IF Category = "Desktop" AND Subcategory = "Printer"
  THEN Assign to "Desktop Support - Printing"
  
IF Category = "Application" AND CI = "SAP"
  THEN Assign to "SAP Support Team"
Rule 2: Priority-Based Routing
IF Priority = "P1" (Critical)
  THEN Assign to "Major Incident Team"
  AND Create conference bridge
  AND Notify IT Manager
  AND Update status page
  
IF Priority = "P2" 
  THEN Assign to senior engineers in appropriate group
Rule 3: VIP User Routing
IF Caller is VIP (C-level, SVP)
  THEN Increase Priority by 1 level
  AND Assign to "VIP Support Team"
  AND Notify support manager
Rule 4: Location-Based Routing
IF Location = "New York Office"
  THEN Assign to "NY Support Team"
  
IF Location = "London Office"
  THEN Assign to "EMEA Support Team"
Rule 5: Skills-Based Routing
IF Subcategory = "SQL Server"
  THEN Assign to agent with "SQL Database" skill
  
IF Description contains "Azure" OR "AWS"
  THEN Assign to agent with "Cloud Infrastructure" skill
Round Robin Assignment:
* Within assignment group, distribute evenly
* Consider agent availability and current workload
* Respect work schedules (don't assign during off-hours)
3.4 Major Incident Management
Major Incident Triggers:
* P1 priority incidents
* Multiple users affected (>100 users)
* Critical service down (email, ERP, etc.)
* Security breach
* Data loss
Major Incident Process:
1. Declaration: Major Incident Manager notified
2. Assembly: Form incident response team (bridge call)
3. Communication:
    * Notify stakeholders
    * Update status page
    * Send regular updates (every 30 minutes)
4. Investigation: Root cause analysis
5. Resolution: Implement fix or workaround
6. Review: Post-incident review within 48 hours
7. Documentation: Capture lessons learned
Major Incident Fields:
* Major Incident (boolean checkbox)
* Bridge Number (conference call details)
* Status Page Updated (boolean)
* Communication Sent (timestamps of updates)
* Incident Commander (lead person)
* Post-Incident Review Scheduled (date/time)
3.5 Incident Escalation
Functional Escalation (to higher skill level):
Tier 1 (Help Desk) 
  ↓ (after 2 hours or 2 reassignments)
Tier 2 (Desktop/App Support)
  ↓ (after 4 hours or complex issue)
Tier 3 (Engineering/Vendor)
Hierarchical Escalation (to management):
Trigger: SLA breach imminent (within 25% of deadline)
Actions:
  1. Notify Assignment Group Manager
  2. If no response in 30 min → Notify IT Director
  3. If P1 and no resolution in 2 hours → Notify CIO
3.6 SLA Tracking
Response SLA:
* P1: 15 minutes
* P2: 1 hour
* P3: 4 hours
* P4: 8 hours (next business day)
Resolution SLA:
* P1: 4 hours
* P2: 8 hours
* P3: 3 business days
* P4: 5 business days
SLA Calculations:
* Business hours only (M-F, 8am-6pm) for P3/P4
* 24/7 for P1/P2
* Pause SLA when state = "On Hold" (awaiting user)
* Resume SLA when state changes back to "In Progress"
SLA Breach Handling:
When SLA at 75%: Warning notification to agent
When SLA at 90%: Escalate to manager
When SLA breached: 
  - Flag incident
  - Automatic escalation to management
  - Report in SLA dashboard
  - Require root cause for breach
3.7 Incident Closure
Closure Requirements:
* Resolution code selected (required)
* Resolution notes entered (min 50 characters)
* Time worked logged
* Caller satisfied (rating provided or auto-close after 3 days)
Auto-Closure Rules:
IF State = "Resolved" 
  AND Days since resolution >= 3
  AND No response from user
THEN 
  Set State = "Closed"
  Set Closed Code = "Auto-closed - No response"
Reopen Logic:
IF Incident closed < 7 days ago
  AND User reports same issue
THEN
  Reopen incident (change state back to "In Progress")
  Add note: "Reopened by user"
  Notify assigned agent
ELSE
  Create new incident
  Link to original as "Related"

