5. Problem Management
5.1 Problem Lifecycle
Problem States:
1. New - Problem identified
2. Assess - Analyzing scope and impact
3. Root Cause Analysis - Investigating underlying cause
4. Fix in Progress - Developing permanent solution
5. Resolved - Solution implemented
6. Closed - Confirmed working, no recurrence
5.2 Problem Fields
Core Fields:
* Number (auto-generated: PRB0001234)
* Short Description
* Description (detailed problem statement)
* Category/Subcategory
* Configuration Item (affected system)
* Priority (based on frequency and impact of related incidents)
Analysis Fields:
* Root Cause (text area - documented cause)
* Workaround (temporary solution while permanent fix in progress)
* Related Incidents (list of incidents caused by this problem)
* Related Changes (changes implementing the fix)
* Known Error (boolean - root cause confirmed)
Investigation Fields:
* Problem Investigator (assigned analyst)
* Problem Manager (manager overseeing)
* Root Cause Analysis Due Date
* Investigation Notes (rich text log)
Resolution Fields:
* Solution (permanent fix description)
* Resolution Code
* Resolved At/By
* Fix Verified (boolean)
* Knowledge Article (link to KB article created from solution)
5.3 Problem Identification
Proactive Problem Identification:
* Trend analysis (multiple similar incidents)
* Monitoring alerts (repeated errors in logs)
* Vendor bulletins (security vulnerabilities)
Reactive Problem Identification:
* Major incident post-review
* User reports of recurring issues
* Multiple incidents with same symptoms
Automatic Problem Creation:
IF Count of incidents with same CI + same Category >= 5 in 7 days
THEN
  Create Problem record
  Link all incidents to problem
  Assign to Problem Management team
  Notify Problem Manager
5.4 Root Cause Analysis
RCA Techniques Documented:
* 5 Whys
* Fishbone Diagram (Ishikawa)
* Fault Tree Analysis
* Timeline Analysis
RCA Template in Problem Record:
Problem Statement:
  Users unable to access SharePoint intermittently

What happened:
  SharePoint site became unresponsive 3 times in past week
  
When did it happen:
  - 3/15 at 2:45 PM for 25 minutes
  - 3/17 at 10:20 AM for 15 minutes  
  - 3/19 at 3:10 PM for 30 minutes

Who was affected:
  All users (500+ people)

Where did it happen:
  SharePoint production server SPRD01

Root Cause (5 Whys):
  1. Why did SharePoint become unresponsive? → IIS app pool crashed
  2. Why did app pool crash? → Memory exhaustion
  3. Why memory exhaustion? → Memory leak in custom web part
  4. Why memory leak? → Event handler not properly disposed
  5. Why not disposed? → Developer didn't follow best practices

Root Cause:
  Custom web part code does not properly dispose of SharePoint objects, causing memory leak

Corrective Action:
  1. Immediate: Restart app pool nightly (workaround)
  2. Short-term: Increase server memory from 16GB to 32GB
  3. Long-term: Fix web part code, deploy via change control

Preventive Action:
  - Implement code review checklist for disposal patterns
  - Add monitoring alert for app pool memory > 80%
  - Schedule web part code audit quarterly
