10. Service Level Management (SLAs)
10.1 SLA Structure
SLA Definition:
* Name (e.g., "Incident Resolution SLA - P1")
* Type (Incident, Request, Change, Problem)
* Conditions (When does this SLA apply? Priority=P1, Category=Critical)
* Schedule (Business Hours or 24/7)
* Target (4 hours for P1 incidents)
SLA Stages:
* Target: The goal time (e.g., 4 hours)
* Warning: Alert before breach (e.g., at 75% = 3 hours)
* Breach: SLA violated
10.2 SLA Definitions by Priority
Incident Resolution SLAs:
Priority	Response Time	Resolution Time	Schedule
P1 (Critical)	15 minutes	4 hours	24/7
P2 (High)	1 hour	8 hours	24/7
P3 (Medium)	4 hours	3 business days	Business Hours
P4 (Low)	8 hours	5 business days	Business Hours
Request Fulfillment SLAs:
Request Type	Approval Time	Fulfillment Time	Schedule
Standard (automated)	N/A	4 hours	24/7
Hardware	1 business day	5 business days	Business Hours
Software	4 hours	2 business days	Business Hours
Access (basic)	4 hours	8 hours	Business Hours
Access (privileged)	1 business day	2 business days	Business Hours
Change SLAs:
Change Type	CAB Approval	Implementation
Standard	Pre-approved	Per schedule
Normal	5 business days	Per plan
Emergency	30 minutes (eCAB)	Immediate
10.3 SLA Pause Conditions
When to Pause SLA:
* Incident/Request state = "On Hold"
* Awaiting User Response
* Awaiting Vendor Response
* Awaiting Third-Party
When to Resume SLA:
* State changes from "On Hold" back to active state
* User responds
* External dependency resolved
Example:
Incident INC0012345 created at 9:00 AM (P2 - 8 hour resolution SLA)
Resolution Due: 5:00 PM (same day)

10:30 AM - Agent requests info from user, sets state to "Awaiting Info"
  → SLA PAUSED at 1.5 hours elapsed, 6.5 hours remaining

2:00 PM - User responds, agent sets state back to "In Progress"
  → SLA RESUMED with 6.5 hours remaining
  → New Due Time: 8:30 PM (2:00 PM + 6.5 hours)

If user had not responded by 5:00 PM original due time, incident would NOT breach
Breach would occur at 8:30 PM if still unresolved
10.4 SLA Breach Handling
Automated Actions on Breach:
1. Flag record with SLA Breach indicator (red)
2. Send notification to assignment group manager
3. Create escalation record
4. Add to breach report
5. Require breach justification note
Breach Retrospective:
* Weekly review of all breaches
* Categorize breach reasons:
    * Insufficient resources
    * Complexity underestimated
    * Awaiting vendor (should have been escalated sooner)
    * Agent error (forgot to work on it)
    * Process gap
* Action plans to prevent recurrence
10.5 SLA Reporting
SLA Dashboards:
* SLA Compliance Rate (% within SLA)
* Breaches by Category
* Breaches by Assignment Group
* Average Time to Resolution vs. Target
* Current At-Risk Tickets (nearing breach)
KPIs:
* SLA Achievement Rate: (Tickets within SLA / Total Tickets) × 100%
    * Target: 95%+
* Average Breach Time: How long past deadline when breaches occur
    * Target: Minimize
* Repeat Breach Offenders: Tickets breached multiple times due to reassignment
    * Target: Zero

